// Example configuration for LLM-based permission checking
// Place this in your project's opencode.json or opencode.jsonc file

{
  // Enable LLM-based permission checking
  "llmPermissionCheck": {
    // Set to true to enable LLM permission checking
    "enabled": true,

    // Optional: specify which model to use for permission checks
    // If not specified, uses the default model from the "model" field
    "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4"
  },

  // Default model for general operations
  "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4",
  "provider": "anthropic",

  // Standard permission rules still apply
  // LLM check is an additional layer on top of these rules
  "permission": {
    // Allow read operations by default
    "read": "allow",
    "grep": "allow",
    "glob": "allow",
    "list": "allow",

    // Ask for edit operations (LLM will additionally check if they're safe)
    "edit": {
      "*": "allow"
    },

    // Allow bash commands (LLM will check if they're safe)
    "bash": {
      "*": "allow"
    },

    // Deny external directory access by default (LLM will enforce this)
    "external_directory": "ask"
  }
}

// How LLM Permission Checking Works:
//
// When enabled, the LLM will evaluate each operation based on these principles:
//
// 1. Read Operations: Always allowed
//    - read, grep, glob, list, etc. will pass LLM check
//
// 2. Modifications Inside Current Directory: Allowed
//    - edit, write, bash commands that modify files in current directory
//
// 3. Modifications Outside Current Directory: Denied
//    - Operations trying to modify/delete files outside current directory
//    - Example: "rm /etc/passwd" will be denied by LLM
//    - Example: "mv ../file ./file" will be denied by LLM
//
// 4. Bash Commands: Analyzed
//    - Safe read commands: "ls", "cat", "grep" → allowed
//    - Modifications inside current dir: "touch file.txt" → allowed
//    - Modifications outside current dir: "rm /etc/passwd" → denied
//    - Uncertain commands → asks user
//
// 5. Script Execution: Deep Analysis
//    - When executing scripts (bash script.sh, python script.py, etc.)
//    - LLM reads the script content and analyzes ALL commands
//    - If ANY command in the script violates security principles → denied
//    - Examples:
//      - Script with "rm file.txt" in current dir → allowed
//      - Script with "rm /etc/passwd" → denied
//      - Script that cannot be read → asks user
//
// 6. Tool/Binary Execution: Conservative Approach
//    - For compiled binaries or unknown tools
//    - LLM cannot inspect the internal behavior
//    - Default behavior: asks user for confirmation
//    - Known safe tools (like common GNU utilities) → allowed
//
// The LLM check is performed AFTER the standard permission rules are evaluated.
// If standard rules deny something, LLM is not consulted.
// If standard rules allow something, LLM provides an additional security layer.
//
// Examples of LLM Security Decisions:
//
// Command: bash deploy.sh (script contains "rm -rf /var/www")
// → LLM denies: modifies files outside current directory
//
// Command: python test.py (script only reads files)
// → LLM allows: safe read operations
//
// Command: ./build (binary tool, unknown behavior)
// → LLM asks: cannot verify safety, user should confirm
//
// Command: npm install
// → LLM asks: package manager might download/execute unknown code
